-so a rollback here is just a redeploy.
+so a rollback is just a redeploy, and it is fast and low-risk.
+
+### Roll back
+
+1. Find the last known-good commit on `main` — the commit before the one that
+   introduced the problem. Confirm its CI run is green (the `CI` workflow, in
+   the Actions tab) before using it; a commit that never passed CI is not "the
+   previous build."
+2. Revert the bad commit and push to `main`:
+   `git revert <bad-commit-sha>` (add `-m 1` if it is a merge commit), then
+   `git push`. Production redeploys automatically on push to `main` — the same
+   path it uses to deploy in the first place (see Environments above) — so
+   rolling back needs no separate release step.
+3. If your host also lets you re-promote a previous deployment directly,
+   skipping the revert-and-push, that works too. Either way, use the checks
+   below to confirm it landed.
+
+### Verify it worked
+
+Do not trust a green deploy pipeline alone — check the site itself:
+
+- Open the production URL and confirm the home page renders: portrait, bio,
+  and post list all present.
+- Open a post that has more than one translation and confirm the right
+  language renders for your browser's locale, then confirm it still falls
+  back correctly for a locale the post has no translation for (see
+  `shared/resolve-translation.ts` for the order it should follow).
+- On your hosting dashboard, confirm the live deployment is now pinned to the
+  reverted commit's SHA, not the one you rolled back from.
+- Check request logs or error monitoring for the few minutes after the
+  rollback. A spike in 4xx/5xx there means the rollback itself introduced a
+  problem, not just failed to fix one.
+
+Treat the incident as unresolved until all of these pass — a green pipeline by
+itself does not mean the rollback worked.
+
+### After the rollback
+
+- If step 1 did not already confirm the reverted commit's CI run was green,
+  run `npm run typecheck`, `npm test`, and `npm run test:e2e` against it
+  locally before trusting it — do not assume an old commit is safe just
+  because it is old.
+- Open an issue (or reopen the one that prompted the rollback) linking the bad
+  commit, the revert commit, and what you observed. This is what the next
+  person reads instead of re-diagnosing from scratch.
+- Leave the bad change reverted on `main` rather than rewriting history to
+  erase it; land the real fix as a new pull request through the normal review
+  process once you are not doing it at 2am.
+
+### Who to notify
+
+This document does not yet name an on-call channel or contact list — add one
+here once your team has one. Until then, at minimum comment on the issue or
+pull request that caused the rollback, so whoever reviews the next change to
+that area sees it; every change here goes through review by someone other
+than its author.
